From 5a0e8bdc63da73783cadca86b076cee2eda7218f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: root <root@localhost>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 18:12:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(http): add --no-proxy-list

A proxy is there to reach hosts that are otherwise unreachable, but some
domains are better fetched directly: Windows Update, driver downloads and
game content come from large, nearby CDNs, and a proxy adds a hop that can
cost the whole speed of the transfer.

"--no-proxy-list" names those domains. It works on top of --no-proxy, so a
configuration that already bypasses a few hosts can add download domains
without touching it; an entry may be "vendor.com" or "*.vendor.com" and
matches the domain and every name below it. A value may repeat, and several
entries may be separated by commas, semicolons or line breaks, as --no-proxy
already allows.

The list is empty by default, so nothing changes for a configuration that
does not use it: --no-proxy keeps the rules it had, and no rules still mean
no bypass. http::noProxyRules() assembles the value CURLOPT_NOPROXY receives
and keeps the meaning --no-proxy always had: "*" still disables the proxy for
every host, and a *.glob still names the host of one request rather than
becoming a pattern libcurl would read literally.
